    "display_name": "Simulating Food Safety",
    "long_instructions": "**Food can go bad fast when it's left out.** \r\n\r\nUnhealthy bacteria multiply quickly in warm food. Scientists measure bacteria in “colony-forming units,” or CFU. Health problems often begin around 100,000 CFU. However, some bacteria can be unhealthy at lower amounts.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n- Update `simulate` so that it simulates how long it will take for food to spoil.\r\n- Ensure that, for each hour, `simulate` prints a message in the format: `\"Hour {hours}: {cfu} CFU\"`.\r\n- Choose and experiment with different arguments to `simulate`. Pay attention to how they change your simulation’s outcome.\r\n\r\n**🖥️ Coding Tip:**\r\n\r\nRemember that Python’s `while` loop can repeat code while a condition is true. For example:\r\n\r\n```\r\ni = 1\r\nwhile i < 5:\r\n    print(i)\r\n    i += 1\r\n```",
